## Synthesis: Patterns, Pricing, and Category Mapping

Clinical patterns. NSAID pages consistently emphasize taking medication after food, avoiding use in specified conditions, and using the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration. Antihistamines (fexofenadine) require attention to absorption interactions (antacids; fruit juices) and storage below 25°C. GLP-1 agonists require injection-site rotation, no pen sharing, and monitoring for pancreatitis and hypoglycemia when combined with insulin. Laxatives (lactulose) follow adult dosing norms with an onset near 48 hours.

Pricing anchors and normalization. Cross-market price tags (EGP vs SAR) are captured and must be normalized in analytics. Anomalies (Duphalac alternative price) will be reconciled via QA source-preference rules.

Category mapping. Controlled vocabularies are stable for antibiotics (penicillin combinations), NSAIDs, GLP-1 RAs, antihistamines, and osmotic laxatives. Subcategory hints improve mapping when clinical fields are sparse.

